#usda 1.0
(
    doc = """Base pump asset for the OPC UA - OpenUSD Bindings pump end-to-end.

    Authored by the '3D / USD asset author' actor (Annex B). It carries only
    geometry and materials; it contains NO live values. The live OPC UA values
    are composed on top by `live.usda` (see `stage.usda`), which a connector
    authors at runtime. The prim paths and attribute names here are the contract
    the OpenUSD bindings target:

      /Plant/Pumps/P101/Impeller       xformOp:rotateZ      (MassFlow -> Rotation)
      /Plant/Pumps/P101/Body           primvars:displayColor(BearingTemperature -> DisplayColor)
      /Plant/Pumps/P101/Body/Mat/Surface.inputs:diffuseColor
                                                            (BearingTemperature -> DisplayColor)
      /Plant/Pumps/P101/StatusLight/Mat/Surface.inputs:emissiveColor
                                                            (DifferentialPressure -> EmissiveColor)
      /Plant/Pumps/P101/StatusLight.visibility              (alarm ActiveState -> Visibility)
      /Plant/Pumps/P101/Impeller.inputs:speedSetpoint       (opt-in command intent -> UA write)

    MODEL
    P101 is a horizontal long-coupled end-suction centrifugal pump built to
    EN 733 (formerly DIN 24255), size 65-200 - the geometry follows the published
    dimensions of the Grundfos NK 65-200 / KSB Etanorm 65-200 family, driven by an
    IEC 160M motor, both bolted to a fabricated steel baseplate. Key dimensions
    (metres): baseplate 1.80 x 0.46 x 0.12; shaft centreline 0.160 above the
    baseplate (the IEC 160M frame number *is* the shaft height in mm); volute
    casing 0.355 outer diameter x 0.110 wide; DN80 axial suction flange (OD 0.200)
    and DN65 vertical discharge flange (OD 0.185) per EN 1092-2 PN16; 0.198
    impeller with six backward-curved vanes; IEC 160M motor frame 0.254 outer
    diameter x 0.615 long. Livery is KSB signal blue (RAL 5005) for the wetted
    castings and RAL 7035 light grey for the motor.

    Two deliberate departures from a real pump, both so the twin can be *seen*:
      * The suction pipe is drawn as a short stub, leaving the casing eye open -
        a cutaway, the way a trade-show display pump is presented - so the
        rotating impeller is visible.
      * The coupling guard is a cage (end rings plus longitudinal bars) rather
        than a solid sheet-metal barrel, so the rotating coupling is visible.

    The shaft of a real pump is horizontal, but the binding contract fixes the
    driven operation as `xformOp:rotateZ`. `Impeller` therefore carries a static
    `xformOp:rotateY = 90` *ahead of* `xformOp:rotateZ` in `xformOpOrder`, which
    lays its local Z along the world shaft axis (X). The contract operation still
    spins the shaft, and it turns the impeller and the coupling together because
    both hang off the same rotating prim - as they do on the real machine.
    """
    defaultPrim = "Plant"
    metersPerUnit = 1
    upAxis = "Z"
)

def Xform "Plant"
{
    def Scope "Looks"
    {
        def Material "PumpBlue"
        {
            token outputs:surface.connect = </Plant/Looks/PumpBlue/Surface.outputs:surface>

            def Shader "Surface"
            {
                uniform token info:id = "UsdPreviewSurface"
                color3f inputs:diffuseColor = (0.02, 0.24, 0.60)
                float inputs:metallic = 0.1
                float inputs:roughness = 0.45
                token outputs:surface
            }
        }

